@Override
    public EnumMap<?,?> deserialize(JsonParser p, DeserializationContext ctxt,
            EnumMap result)
        throws JacksonException
    {
        // [databind#631]: Assign current value, to be accessible by custom deserializers
        p.assignCurrentValue(result);

        String keyStr;
        if (p.isExpectedStartObjectToken()) {
            keyStr = p.nextName();
        } else {
            JsonToken t = p.currentToken();
            if (t != JsonToken.PROPERTY_NAME) {
                if (t == JsonToken.END_OBJECT) {
                    return result;
                }
                ctxt.reportWrongTokenException(this, JsonToken.PROPERTY_NAME, null);
            }
            keyStr = p.currentName();
        }
        if (keyStr == null) {
            return result;
        }

        // Need to resolve ObjectIds (forward refs)?
        final MapDeserializer.MapReferringAccumulator referringAccumulator =
            (_valueDeserializer.getObjectIdReader(ctxt) == null)
                    ? null
                    : new MapDeserializer.MapReferringAccumulator(
                            _containerType.getContentType().getRawClass(), result);

        for (; keyStr != null; keyStr = p.nextName()) {
            // but we need to let key deserializer handle it separately, nonetheless
            Enum<?> key = (Enum<?>) _keyDeserializer.deserializeKey(keyStr, ctxt);
            JsonToken t = p.nextToken();
            if (key == null) {
                if (!ctxt.isEnabled(EnumFeature.READ_UNKNOWN_ENUM_VALUES_AS_NULL)) {
                    return (EnumMap<?,?>) ctxt.handleWeirdStringValue(_enumClass, keyStr,
                            "value not one of declared Enum instance names for %s",
                            ClassUtil.getTypeDescription(_containerType.getKeyType()));
                }
                // 24-Mar-2012, tatu: Null won't work as a key anyway, so let's
                //  just skip the entry then. But we must skip the value as well, if so.
                p.skipChildren();
                continue;
            }
            // And then the value...
            // note: MUST check for nulls separately: deserializers will
            // not handle them (and maybe fail or return bogus data)
            Object value;

            try {
                if (t == JsonToken.VALUE_NULL) {
                    if (_skipNullValues) {
                        continue;
                    }
                    value = null;
                } else {
                    value = _deserializeNoNullChecks(p, ctxt);
                }

                if (value == null) {
                    value = _nullProvider.getNullValue(ctxt);

                    if (value == null && _skipNullValues) {
                        continue;
                    }
                }
                if (referringAccumulator == null) {
                    result.put(key, value);
                } else {
                    referringAccumulator.put(key, value);
                }
            } catch (UnresolvedForwardReference reference) {
                _handleUnresolvedReference(ctxt, referringAccumulator, key, reference);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                return wrapAndThrow(ctxt, e, result, keyStr);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
```

## Specification (intended behavior)
Functional Description: Deserializes JSON objects into an EnumMap, mapping enum keys to their corresponding values. It handles various scenarios such as null values, unknown enum keys, and forward references, ensuring that the resulting EnumMap is populated correctly based on the provided JSON structure.

Args:
p: JsonParser - The parser used to read JSON tokens and extract data for deserialization.
ctxt: DeserializationContext - The context for deserialization, providing configuration and error handling capabilities.
result: EnumMap - The EnumMap instance that will be populated with deserialized key-value pairs.

Returns: EnumMap<?,?> - The populated EnumMap containing enum keys mapped to their corresponding values extracted from the JSON input.

Preconditions:
1. The JsonParser must be properly initialized and positioned at the start of a JSON object.
2. The EnumMap must be instantiated and passed as the result parameter.
3. The key and value deserializers must be properly configured and initialized.

Postconditions:
1. The result EnumMap will contain key-value pairs corresponding to the deserialized JSON object.
2. The state of the JsonParser will be advanced to the end of the processed JSON object.
3. Any unknown enum keys will be handled according to the configuration in the DeserializationContext.

Invariants:
1. The method will always return the result EnumMap, which may be empty if no valid entries are found.
2. Null values are handled according to the configuration of the _skipNullValues field.
3. The method is idempotent; calling it multiple times with the same input will yield the same result.

Exception:
JacksonException - If an unexpected token is encountered or if deserialization fails due to issues with the input data or configuration.

